    <p class="note"><a href="/guides">Guides</a> / Developer basics</p>
    <h1>What Is a Computer Date Number? A Plain Guide</h1>
    <p class="lead">Many computers store a date as one large number: the count of seconds since 1 January 1970. This guide explains that number and why you might see it.</p>
    <div class="ad-slot" aria-label="Advertisement"></div>
<h2>One number for one moment</h2>
<p>Computers often store a date as the number of seconds since 1 January 1970 at midnight in London. This is called a Unix timestamp. It is easy for a computer to store and compare, even though it is hard for a person to read.</p>
<h2>How to read one</h2>
<p>A timestamp like 1,793,004,000 is a moment in time. Converting it gives a readable date and time, such as 26 October 2026 at 08:40, in UTC (the same time everywhere in the world, without daylight saving).</p>
<h2>Why you might see one</h2>
<p>You may see these numbers in a web address, a file's details or an app's data. The converter turns them back into dates.</p>
      <section class="worked">
        <h2>Worked example</h2>
        <p>The number 1,793,004,000 counts the seconds since 1 January 1970. That moment is 26 October 2026 at 08:40 UTC, which is the same time everywhere in the world.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="faq-list">
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <details><summary>How can I tell a seconds number from a milliseconds number?</summary><p>A seconds number for today has 10 digits. A milliseconds number has 13.</p></details>
        <details><summary>What is UTC?</summary><p>It is a standard world time with no daylight saving changes.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Is there a problem coming in 2038?</summary><p>Older systems that store the number in a limited space stop working on 19 January 2038. Most current devices are not affected.</p></details>
      </section>
